Parrot is the new execution backend for sBPF.

This work started with the realization that BPF instruction structure is amenable to interpreters
that have much less overhead than the current state-of-art in the ecosystem.  Until Parrot the
fastest interpreters would maintain a table of handler functions to which the execution would
tail-call (jump) as it processes each new instruction. In pseudo-code:

```rust
fn dispatch() {
    let next_handler = table[opcode];
    become next_handler();
}
```

The first observation we had that this involves two dependent memory operations before the code for
the next instruction can start executing: first the load of the `next_handler` address, and then
load of the machine code inside `next_handler` itself. I figured that maybe we could "inline" the
machine code into this table and jump to the right location right away, without the level of
indirection. This way determining the next part of the machine code to execute would be easily
pipelineable, predictable and only involve cheap arithmetic operations:

```rust
fn dispatch() {
    const TABLE_BASE: *const u8; // base address at which handlers for instructions begin
    const STEP: usize = 128; // each table element has capacity for 128 bytes of machine code
    let next_handler_addr = TABLE_BASE + opcode * STEP;
    become next_handler_addr();
}
```

The only downside to this new approach is that each step occupies full 128 bytes of memory (in this
example), even though the machine code for most instructions can be much more compact. Still, with
only 256 opcodes (the opcode field in eBPF isntruction is a u8), that's just 32kiB of memory!

This observation directly led us to a solution for another problem we were having with traditional
interpreters: handling of the register fields. Parsing the 4-bit register fields at each step can
become quite expensive; not to mention that runtime handling of register indices forces register
data to be stored in memory and a bounds check on every access to that memory, because there are
only 11 registers, but the 4-bit field can contain 16 distinct values. We figured that we can
roll-up all of these concerns into the same instruction dispatch logic: dispatch on the first 16
bits of the instruction to a containing handlers for every possible 65536 bit combination in those
bits[^1]. With this in place a handler for `mov r14, r0` can directly generate an "invalid
instruction" exception without any additional checks (for we know that r14 is not a valid
instruction when we build the table.) On the other hand code to process `mov r0, r0` can statically
omit the actual move because and most importantly the interpreter now gains the super-power of
being able to map the registers in BPF code to native machine registers!

[^1]: making the table grow to still very reasonable 8MiB (for 128 byte steps).

And suddenly the code executed by the interpreter is not *that* different from what we'd generate
with a JIT compiler for the same eBPF code. The main difference is really just the presence of the
dispatch epilogue after each step's implementation to jump to the next instruction to process.

We ended up arriving at an implementation strategy for JIT called [copy-and-patch] by almost a
natural coincidence. We wanted to reuse the same sort of dispatch logic as we had devised for the
interpreter to determine the machine code to produce into the JIT. Instruction registers are
already part of the dispatch, so it seemed natural to pre-generate a table templates that we'd
later just copy over into the output buffer. This memcpy JIT seemed to work great until we had to
implement instructions that inspect the offset (branches) and immediate parts of the instruction.
We had to figure out how to vary – patch – each template of machine instructions we've been copying
over just enough for it to be able to read the offset and immediate parts of the original BPF
instruction. The result is a JIT compiler that is as fast at compiling BPF programs as the
interpreter at interpreting them. The ceiling for generated machine code quality is perhaps not as
high as for some other JIT code generation approaches, but in direct comparison with the previous
implementation of JIT, the new implementation is still producing similarly good machine code while
being 2-5 times faster to compile the same program.

A nice benefit of sharing the underlying logic between JIT and interpreter is that the execution
state between the two is exactly the same. This makes it relatively easier to have confidence that
the two different modes of execution will produce exactly the same outcome. In fact, the
implementation of the two modes in Parrot is so similar that with very small changes it would be
possible to switch execution between the modes on the fly with very little additional effort.

[copy-and-patch]: https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Copy-and-patch
